// TAS-078: the Increment 1 narrow isolated curve fixture.
//
// Two constant-curvature bikeways, one per narrow mode, authored as concentric
// quarter circles of radius 80 m (bicycle) and 86 m (scooter) about the origin,
// so neither mode constrains the other and both ride a genuinely turning
// reference path. Each guide path is the chord polyline of its arc — the only
// reference-path form `paths[].points` authors — and the facility's
// `reference_path` is that polyline, so the run follows the same curved geometry
// the compiled facility exposes.
//
// The arc each polyline is the chord approximation of is declared here as the
// facility's analytic constant-curvature reference, one per mode:
//
// | path | centre | radius | start angle | sweep |
// | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
// | `bicycle_arc_r80` | `(0, 0)` | 80 m | `-pi/2` | `+pi/2` (counter-clockwise) |
// | `scooter_arc_r86` | `(0, 0)` | 86 m | `-pi/2` | `+pi/2` (counter-clockwise) |
//
// so a vertex at index `i` of `2 * 24` equals
// `(r * cos(-pi/2 + (pi/2) * i / 24), r * sin(-pi/2 + (pi/2) * i / 24))`. The
// fixture test reconstructs that analytic reference with the compiled
// `CompiledReferencePath::arc` geometry and holds it to `T-RT`; the authored
// polyline vertices are exactly that arc's chord points, so the compiled
// facility and the declared analytic reference describe one curve.
//
// Both templates author constant (min == max) bodies and desired speeds, so the
// sampled profile is the authored value (`T-DIM`, `T-SPD`). Demand rates keep
// successive agents far enough apart that no narrow agent ever follows a
// leader, so the run is an isolated free-flow curve — the Increment 1 "isolated
// curve" rung. This is `CC-NARROW` evidence: every cell it feeds stays at the
// matrix's `F/S/f` presets.
